   "text": "\n\n## How a Hash Table Handles Collisions\n\nA hash table maps keys to values using a hash function that converts a key into an index in an array. Collisions occur when two different keys produce the same index. Since hash functions are not injective (many keys map to the same range of indices), collisions are inevitable, especially as the table fills up.\n\nThere are two main strategies for handling collisions:\n\n### 1. Chaining\nEach bucket in the array holds a linked list (or another collection) of all key-value pairs that hash to that index. When a collision occurs, the new entry is simply appended to the list.\n\n- **Pros:** Simple to implement; deletion is straightforward; load factor can exceed 1.\n- **Cons:** Pointer overhead; cache-unfriendly due to scattered memory; worst-case lookup degrades to O(n) if all keys collide.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing\nAll entries are stored directly in the array. When a collision occurs, the algorithm probes for the next available slot using a deterministic sequence. Common probing schemes include:\n\n- **Linear probing:** Check index i, then i+1, i+2, \u2026 (mod table size).\n- **Quadratic probing:** Check i"
